TAM-VIEW - A TERMINAL FILE VIEWER

tam-view reads text files and piped input. It borrows its navigation
from less and leaves the last page visible when you press q. At end of
file it shows a message and waits for q (pass -E / --quit-at-eof to
return automatically at EOF). File text starts at the top of the
terminal, with blank rows below short files and short final pages.
The shell prompt appears at the bottom when the viewer exits.

Full-page navigation repeats two displayed rows for context in either
direction. Use --overlap N (or --overlap=N) to set the number of repeated
rows. With --overlap 0, the next page starts immediately after the previous
page's last row. Wrapped lines count by their displayed rows, and a page
always moves by at least one row when more content is available.

Interactive named files are watched by default. Appends keep the last
line visible when it was already on screen. Scroll up to read earlier
lines, then use G or End to return to the last page. Edits refresh the
view and keep the same text in view where possible. Use --no-watch to
read a static snapshot taken when each file is first opened. Watch
mode leaves piped input and redirected output behavior as before.

Follows the tam-idol 1.1.0 standard.


BUILD AND TRY

  Needs a C11 compiler, Make, and libtam 0.13.0 or later, whose
  libtam-render archive holds the document source, layout and
  Markdown/HTML/Mermaid renderers. LIBTAM_PREFIX defaults to
  $HOME/.local. The binary links libc and the static libtam-render and
  libtam archives.

    make
    make test
    make sanitize
    build/tam-view README
    build/tam-view -r README.md
    build/tam-view -R --ascii ROADMAP.md
    build/tam-view --no-color sandbox/demo.md
    build/tam-view README ROADMAP.md
    printf 'one\ntwo\n' | build/tam-view
    build/tam-view --raw -- filename

  make install and make uninstall use PREFIX (default $HOME/.local).
  DESTDIR lets a packager stage the files. make clean removes build/.


KEYS

  Down, j              Next line (or move attention line / next link in Markup)
  Up, k                Previous line (or move attention line / prev link in Markup)
  Space, Page Down     Next page
  b, Page Up           Previous page
  d, u                 Half page down or up
  Right, Enter, o      Follow highlighted link (Right scrolls if no link)
                       External links require a second press.
  Left, Backspace      Return in link history (Left scrolls if shifted)
  Tab, Shift-Tab       Jump to next / previous link in document
  Shift-Left, -Right   Scroll horizontally
  S                    Toggle chop long lines
  Home, <, 1g          Start of file
  G, End               Show the last page
  Ng                   Go to file line N, for example 42g
  m<a-z>, '<a-z>, ''   Set mark, go to mark, or return to previous position
  /regex, ?regex       Find POSIX regex forward or backward
  n, N                 Repeat the search or reverse its direction
  :n, :p, :x, :f       Next, previous, first, or current file in list
  R                    Toggle rendered / raw view
  C                    Toggle color and search highlighting
  F                    Follow growing watched file or pipe (Ctrl-C stops)
  Esc, Ctrl-C          Cancel a search/follow, or clear search highlight
  Ctrl-Z               Suspend and restore the shell's tty settings
  g, h                 Toggle the keybindings guide
  q                    Quit and keep the visible text

  Searches are case-sensitive. Enter submits a query and Backspace
  removes a character. Redirected output is a byte-for-byte copy
  unless -R/--render is passed (plain text unless --color is also set).
  Color respects --color[=auto|always|never], --no-color, NO_COLOR,
  TERM=dumb, and TERM=linux.
  OSC 8 terminal hyperlinks respect --hyperlinks[=auto|always|never] and
  --no-hyperlinks (omitted on TERM=linux, TERM=dumb, --no-color,
  NO_COLOR, and redirected stdout by default).
  Rendered Markdown and HTML show an attention line for link navigation
  and a footer with <path> | L<line> <top>-<bot>/<total> vX.Y.Z; plain
  text and raw views scroll directly with Up/Down.
  The viewer uses the primary screen on the Linux console and
  ANSI terminals such as tam-last. Scrollback, where available, is
  maintained by the terminal.


WHERE THINGS ARE

  src/, include/   the pager, source access, and text layout
  tests/           layout, pseudoterminal, and CLI checks
  arch/            how input, layout, and display fit together
  ideas/           behavior learned from less and more
  plans/           text and markup milestones
  bugs/, reviews/  numbered records
  work/            WHATNOW and work session records
  ROADMAP.md       versions and milestones
  UPSTREAM.md      upstream code ledger
  COPYRIGHT        copyright and license notices


